WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:29.520
[A bell rings and sounds of traffic are heard.]

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.920
[George:] It's been nice all week. It'll probably rain 
all weekend.

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.680
[Friend:] No, it's gonna be beautiful and I'm gonna 
give you six strokes and still take all your money.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.640
[George:] Leave me out, guys. I had a bad week 
and I'm just gonna rest.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.800
[The Guys:] Huh?

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.640
[Second friend:] Come on George. You haven't 
been out in weeks.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.800
[George:] Well, to tell you truth, I've been thinking 
of...

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:54.240
[Second friend:] Oh, you're getting to be an old 
man.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.040
[George:] Ha, getting to be an old man.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:03.200
Ha. Ah, I don't know.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.400
Maybe it's true.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.400
I'm feeling blah.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.640
Good evening, Mrs. Green.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.800
Well, the whole weekend to do just what I want to 
do, nothing.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.600
Mrs. Orange.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.080
Unless Ethel's got one of her lists again.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.120
Well, it won't take long to find out.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:31.360
[Ethel:] After dinner, George, I was thinking, there 
are a few things I'd like to take care of.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.840
[George:] Well, why don't we wait until after 
dinner?

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.940
I want to rest. I want to take the weekend off.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.680
[George's Son:] On four, hut, hut, hut, hut.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:44.160
Hi.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.200
[George:] Hey.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.560
[George's son:] Hey, is this gonna work?

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.480
[George:] Sure, if you work it right.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.360
[George's Son:]  A delayed reverse strong side 
sweep of five, three, split left option.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:56.720
[George:] How does he flunk?

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.520
[Ethel:] Chow!

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.800
[George:] Ahhhhhh. Da-da-da-dum!

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.280
[George's son:] Wow.
[George's daughter:] Gee.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.160
[Ethel:] George!

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.520
[George's son:] Fumble.
[George's daughter:] Daddy.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.760
[Ethel:] Pass the duck to your father, dear.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.840
What is the matter with you, George?

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.680
[George:] Nothing. I slipped. I feel fine.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.240
[Ethel:] I've been making a list, George.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.080
We've got a busy weekend. It's going to be split-
second timing.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.440
Now, while I'm doing the dishes, you run right 
around the corner to the hardware store, and get...

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.640
[Crowd cheering]

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:40.720
[Crowd:] Go, George, go! Go, George, go! Go, 
George, go! Go, George, go! Go, George, go!

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.720
Go, George, go! Go, George, go! Yay!

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.800
[Ethel:] Well, why didn't you say something about 
it?

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.880
You used to enjoy working around the house.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:53.760
[George:] I do.

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.600
[Ethel:] Well, you ought to go see a doctor today.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.120
[George:] I'm not sick. Gotta go!

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:58.720
[Ethel:] Your toast!

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.000
[George:] Gotta hurry.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.840
Gotta slow down.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.440
Boy, it's been hanging on.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.400
Glad it's Monday, got a chance to take it easy.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.680
[Co-worker:] Why don't you keep these, Mr. Brown. 
I never get headaches.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.280
[George:] Oh, no, no, just two is all I need.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.480
[Co-worker:] Oh, look, go ahead and keep them.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.000
My grandmother's got arthritis, and she takes them 
all the time.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.280
I'll see you.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.520
[George:] Arthritis?

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:32.160
So I think maybe I'm getting a touch of arthritis.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:32.800
[Dr. Clyde:] Let's have a look.

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.200
[George:] I know you're busy, and I didn't make an 
appointment.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.480
[Dr. Clyde:] How long has it been bothering you?

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.400
[George:] Ohhh! Well, a few months off and on.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.280
[Dr. Clyde:] In the morning when you get up?

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.320
[George:] Yeah.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.720
Yeah, usually. Ahhhh.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.720
[Dr. Clyde:] Uh-huh. How long does it take to go 
away?

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.080
[George:] Not long.

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.200
[Dr. Clyde:] Taking aspirin?

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.520
[George:] Yes. Yeah, yes.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.040
[Dr. Clyde:] Good, now get dressed.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.840
Arthritis is from the Greek, "arthron," joint, "itis," 
inflammation.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:03.760
Pathogenesis can be metabolic, infectious, 
traumatogenic, congenital, or obscure.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.560
Differential diagnosis determines prognosis and 
optimum therapeutics.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.640
Symptomatic relief is advisable.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.760
Initially, radical pharmacology is contraindicated.

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:18.560
Mitigation can be achieved by salicylates. Aspirin.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.960
[George:] Doc, that's over my head.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.160
[Dr. Clyde:] I see. Well, let's make an appointment 
next time, okay.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:27.840
Tuesday, two weeks? We'll make some tests and 
really get into it.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.080
Oh, Miss Ferbel, give Mr. Brown Diet 3, please.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.520
I'll see you in two weeks, George.

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.360
[Miss Ferbel:] Here you are, Mr. Brown.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.240
[George:] Yeah, here I am. Thanks.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.520
Aspirin, a diet, and a lot of big words.

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.400
[Man on the street:] Doctors, they don't know what 
to do about arthritis.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.640
[George:] Well, he seemed to know a lot about it.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.000
[Woman on the street:] Did he do you any good?

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.880
[George:] Well, what else is there?

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.800
Does anybody know what to do?

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.720
[Man's voice:] Sassafras tea. You ought to try 
sassafras tea.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.880
[George:] Get serious, will you?

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.480
[Man's voice:] Don't knock it until you try it.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.960
[Another male voice:] A good stiff drink'll fix it.

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:02.400
It's good for what ails you.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.080
[George:] Yeah.

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.480
[Various male voices:] Complete rest. Do 
absolutely nothing.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.920
You might try ice packs.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:14.160
Why don't you start lifting weights?

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.320
[Woman's voice:]  Aspirin.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:18.480
[Man's voice:] Opium.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.000
[Woman:] Yoga.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.600
[Skeptical man:] I think it's all in your mind.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:24.400
[George:] Huh?

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:25.360
[Skeptical man:] Psychosomatic.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.080
[George:] Now look, buddy, I know when my arm hurts.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.320
[Skeptical man:] Take it easy, it's just an opinion.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:31.360
[George:] Who asked you?

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.520
[Skeptical man:] I thought you did.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.160
[George:] Everybody's got an opinion, even Ethel.

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.560
"Go back to the doctor," she said.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.360
What for? Take two aspirin, and keep your chin up.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.960
Well, if it doesn't get any worse, I'll be okay.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.920
Ah, that dirty, oh, for heaven's, I don't know.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.600
[Sound of lamp breaking]

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.600
[George's daughter:] Whatcha doing in the dark?

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.440
[George:] I'm just thinking, honey.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:01.040
[George's daughter:] Can you fix my puppet?

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:02.640
[George:] Sure.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.040
[George's daughter:] Thank you.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:15.600
[George:] Hmmm. Hmmm. Hmmm.

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.120
[Ethel:] George, for heaven's sake.

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:20.160
[George:] I'm going.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:21.920
[Ethel:] Don't slam the door.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.400
[Dr. Clyde:] George Brown. Come in. It's been a 
while.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.160
[George:] Yeah, well, I missed the appointment. It 
wasn't bothering me at the time.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.680
[Dr. Clyde:] Uh-huh, remission of symptoms.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:30.880
[George:] Hold it, hold it, Doc.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.200
There are enough words I don't understand 
around here already.

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:34.960
[Dr. Clyde:] I see.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:36.720
[George:] Anyway, the elbow is worse.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.360
Hands are beginning to get stiff in the knuckles, you know.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.520
Now what's going on?

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.720
[Dr. Clyde:] Miss Ferbel, no calls please.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.600
Well, let's see, you're easily fatigued.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:48.080
[George:] Yeah.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:49.680
[Dr. Clyde:] And you regularly woke up stiff and 
sore.

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:50.240
[George:] Yeah.

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:52.720
[Dr. Clyde:] And your elbow and now your knuckles 
are inflamed and painful.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:54.320
[George:] That's me. I've got arthritis.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:56.480
[Dr. Clyde:] Well, it's not that simple.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.400
The word "arthritis" covers all the disorders that 
affect the joints of the body.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:05.540
They've been around since the year one, plaguing 
every generation of mankind.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.520
Gradually, we learned that there are dozens of 
really different disorders that show up in the joints.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:17.360
So, for somebody who is regularly stiff and sore 
someplace, here's the first question:

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:18.960
"Just what have I got?"

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:20.480
[George:] Okay. That's my question.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:24.480
[Dr. Clyde:] Well, I can give you a quick opinion, 
George, but we'll make some tests to be sure.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:26.080
There are a lot of possibilities.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:27.600
Here's the most familiar type.

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:29.600
[George:] Osteoarthritis.

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:34.840
[Dr. Clyde:] Right. This is the one that makes most 
people think arthritis is an old folks' disease.

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:36.560
Here's what happens.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:41.200
A joint has, let's say, a normal shape and works 
beautifully because of it.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:44.560
Osteoarthritis is a change in that shape.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:50.240
Little by little, cartilage deteriorates in the lining 
and new bone grows around the edges.

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:55.200
Many things seem to be able to start this 
remodeling, so even though it seems

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:59.760
to be connected just with the wear and tear of a lifetime,  well, that isn't the whole story.

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:05.040
Even in a young person, osteoarthritis can begin if 
the joint is under constant strain,

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:09.200
like tennis elbow or housemaid's knee.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:11.040
[George:] Housewife's jaw?

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.040
[Dr. Clyde:] Not usually.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:18.400
Anyway, many people technically have 
osteoarthritis and don't even know it,

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:23.040
because it begins slowly in only a few joints and 
doesn't get inflamed.

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:26.000
It's not usually as serious as say, gout.

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.280
[George:] Gout? That's that funny thing, isn't it?

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:31.440
[Dr. Clyde:] Well, here's the traditional picture.

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:41.360
[Music and giggling]

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:46.000
The trouble is, the traditional picture is wrong.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.360
You can live very sensibly and still have gout.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:50.400
It's a metabolic disease.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:51.280
[George:] Ah, ah, ah, ah.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.280
[Dr. Clyde:] I'm sorry. In other words, the body's 
chemistry is out of balance.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:01.360
There's too much of one chemical, uric acid, and 
little crystals form in the joint linings,

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:03.200
often right here.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.320
Now suddenly, there's an attack, and it's not funny.

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.320
It causes some terrific pain.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:13.560
But we've got some very reliable drugs to stop 
attacks, and if we know someone has gout,

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:19.600
we can even prevent them with drugs that control 
the uric acid and restore the chemical balance.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.000
And then there's rheumatoid arthritis.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.360
It's really more than a local joint disorder.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:31.520
It spreads, inflames a number of joints in the body, 
affects the general health.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:38.000
Some doctors think it's an infectious disease 
caused by an agent, like a germ or virus,

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:41.040
so we're trying to find out who he is.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.280
There's another theory that this agent is 
responsible only for the first step

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:47.200
of a more complex process.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:53.440
The theory is that normal cells in and around the 
joint are changed by this agent just enough

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:57.760
to make them strangers to the body's defense 
forces in the bloodstream.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.320
So the defenders, tricked by the change of 
uniform,

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:05.120
attack them like they would an invader from 
outside.

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:09.040
There's a lot of research going on in these 
theories, and now there's two.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:15.040
All of it's aimed at understanding the causes of the 
disease so we can get a cure.

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:19.760
But for now, I'm afraid the picture isn't exactly clear.

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:21.200
[George:] Have I got it?

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.520
[Dr. Clyde:] I can't say yet, but it's possible.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:25.920
[George:] One of the those rare things, huh?

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:28.960
[Dr. Clyde:] Rare? No. About three or four million 
cases in the country.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.600
All together, all types of arthritis, over 16 million.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.800
[George:] No kidding. 16 million.
[Dr. Clyde:] Um-hum.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:39.200
[George:] And all of us are stumbling around 
gulping aspirin and hurting like the devil.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:42.160
There's got to be a cure. Somebody must have 
found something.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:43.840
[Dr. Clyde:] If you insist on believing that.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:46.480
Let me show you just what you're in for.

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.320
You won't need much money to begin with, you can 
start with nice, simple things,

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.320
like a copper bracelet.

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.320
Maybe a warm watchband.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:58.960
Spend only 15 minutes a day with the 
electromagnetic vitalizer.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:01.520
Then try a little local vibrator.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:03.620
Graduate to the heavy-duty model.

00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:07.060
Forget your pain under the amazing Spectronic V-
Ray.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:12.400
Take your pick of a hundred fantastic, miraculous 
brand new time-tested formulas,

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:17.040
or go sit in a helpful old uranium mine.

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:23.040
[George, coughing:] Doc, you don't think I'd fall for all that.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:26.000
[Dr. Clyde:] Lots of people do, George. It's a 300 million dollar business.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:30.080
[George:] Wow. Well, what about those people who 
say they've been cured?

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:31.440
[Dr. Clyde:] Testimonials.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:35.520
Arthritis is a screwy disease, erratic.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:40.560
Sometimes the symptoms will just stop for a few 
weeks, a few years, and if you were using

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:44.320
moose grease about that time, you'd think it was a 
miracle drug.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:49.600
But then, some morning you'll wake up and find 
you spent years here,

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:52.560
and your pain is back now, worse.

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:54.160
And you're getting crippled.

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:56.640
[George:] Doc, you're not making my day, you 
know what I mean?

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.400
[Dr. Clyde:] Well, George, now...

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:01.280
[George:] Now, wait a minute. I come in here, you 
tell me, I've got it. It's going to get worse.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.320
I might be a cripple, and nothing's gonna help.

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:07.280
[Dr. Clyde:] Sit down a minute, George.
[George:] No, go ahead.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:10.560
[Dr. Clyde:] There are two false notions about 
arthritis.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:11.360
One is, there's a quick cure.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:15.600
But there's another: there's nothing to do but 
suffer.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:20.560
Now that's false, too, and dangerous, because just 
about the worst thing

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:23.140
you can do about arthritis is nothing.

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:26.640
While you sit there feeling sorry for yourself, it's 
getting worse, and that's just what

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:27.320
you want to prevent.

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:28.560
[George:] What can I do?

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.520
[Dr. Clyde:] Well, you make up your mind.

00:12:30.520 --> 00:12:32.240
You've got a chronic disease.

00:12:32.240 --> 00:12:36.520
You're going to have to deal with it for a long time, 
maybe all your life.

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:38.520
But we've learned a lot that can help you.

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:39.820
[George:] Well, fill me in.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:41.840
[Dr. Clyde:] You're already using one thing, aspirin.

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:42.640
[George:] Oh, well.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:45.600
[Dr. Clyde:] Now, don't underrate it because it's 
common.

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:48.240
With the dose increased to high level, it's pretty 
powerful.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:51.680
It reduces pain and swelling and inflammation.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:54.800
It's the basic drug as long as it does the job.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:58.400
Later, if we need them, there are some even more 
powerful drugs.

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:00.080
[George:] I knew there must be something.

00:13:00.080 --> 00:13:02.240
[Dr. Clyde:] Yes, but it's not that easy.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:06.720
These drugs, steroids, gold, affect each case 
differently.

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:13.200
It might take a while to judge the success of a 
drug, and there can be some bad side effects.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:20.080
So, we begin conservatively, advance slowly, and 
watch carefully.

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:22.320
Another thing we watch is what you eat.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:25.200
[George:] There was nothing special about that 
diet you gave me.

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:28.560
[Dr. Clyde:] No, no gimmicks, just good nutrition 
and proper weight

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:30.160
to give those bones a fighting chance.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:34.880
Now, you can't handle arthritis just by putting the 
right things in your mouth.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:37.360
There's physical medicine in the program, too.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:40.000
Here's the theory behind it.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:46.000
If a sore joint is favored too much, if you retire it, 
the surrounding muscles get weaker

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.160
and the whole thing drifts apart.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:54.720
In time, the space in the joint gets filled in with 
tissue, and when that hardens, you're crippled.

00:13:55.120 --> 00:13:56.800
[George:] So I've got to keep it working.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:00.080
[Dr. Clyde:] Right. The motto is, "If you can, do."

00:14:00.080 --> 00:14:03.120
[George:] Yeah, well, that's my wife's motto, too. I'm 
pretty active.

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:06.000
[Dr. Clyde:] Good. But don't just grit your teeth and 
go on.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.380
You've got to keep the pain and inflammation 
down.

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:09.780
[George:] Good thought.

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:11.380
[Dr. Clyde:] The one thing that helps is heat.

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:12.480
[George:] Yeah, I noticed that.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:18.780
[Dr. Clyde:] Okay. Dry heat, wet heat, and special 
treatments, like hot paraffin baths.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:22.380
Each has its advantages, and they do more than 
soothe pain.

00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:26.920
They make a physical therapy routine easier and 
more helpful.

00:14:26.980 --> 00:14:29.700
Suppose a certain set of muscles needs toning up.

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:32.440
The routines are tailored to accomplish that.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:39.340
Or if the joint is overstrained, there are all sorts of 
devices designed to rest it, or reinforce it.

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.300
So there's a lot we can do.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:47.840
The best program for an individual takes 
cooperation, the patient, his doctor, and his family.

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:51.960
Each has a part, but the patient is the key.

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:55.760
If he lets himself feel like an invalid, his arthritis can 
make him one.

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:01.380
But if he starts a treatment program early and 
sticks to it, arthritis won't keep him

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:05.400
from leading a pretty normal life, active, satisfying.

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:07.780
What do you think, George?

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:13.560
[George:] I think he should keep his head down. 
And I guess I'll keep mine up.

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:14.980
[Dr. Clyde:] Let's make these tests.

00:15:14.980 --> 00:15:17.160
[George:] You know, I don't keep mine down all the 
time, either.

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:18.840
And the grip's terrible.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:20.300
How about this physical therapy?

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:23.540
I've got some muscles that keep forgetting I'm 
trying to play golf, you know.

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:26.520
[Music]

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:27.640
Evening, Mrs. Green.

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:30.900
[Narrator:] George Brown has had a hard time 
getting to know

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:33.240
what he needed to know about arthritis.

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:38.060
It took him a year to learn what you just learned in 
15 minutes.

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:43.160
Now, if you'll just keep the facts straight, it could 
save a lot of time and a lot of pain,

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:49.220
if you should ever happen to become one of those 
16 million Americans who have arthritis.

00:15:49.220 --> 00:16:22.740
[Music]

